from lib import *

def build(doc):
    h1(doc, "Chapter 2  Conceptual Framework")
    para(doc, "The framework joins three bodies of theory that the project brief names: transaction cost economics, supply-chain visibility and resilience, and blockchain traceability. Each yields testable propositions that the later chapters measure with Bloomberg data or model.", 10.5, 6)
    h2(doc, "2.1  Transaction cost economics in procurement")
    para(doc, "Transaction cost economics (TCE) explains governance choices by the cost of running exchange: searching, negotiating, monitoring and enforcing (Williamson, 1979, 1985). Costs rise with asset specificity, uncertainty and the difficulty of measuring what was delivered. Palm-oil procurement scores high on the last two. A buyer cannot inspect origin by looking at the oil, and compliance depends on facts (land clearing history, plot boundaries) that sit with distant third parties.")
    para(doc, "This is Akerlof’s (1970) “market for lemons” in sustainability form. If compliant and non-compliant oil look identical and the buyer cannot verify origin, the market pays one blended price, and honest producers cannot earn a premium for the cost of proving compliance. Buyers respond with audits, certification and questionnaires, which add *auditing friction*: repeated, partly duplicated checks across tiers. The cost of verifying grows with the number of plots and intermediaries, while the penalty for failure (EUDR fines, market-access loss) is now paid by the buyer. The TCE prediction is that buyers will move toward governance forms that lower measurement cost, such as shared registries, vertical integration or long-term relational contracts, when the regulatory price of ignorance exceeds the price of measurement.")
    para(doc, "**Proposition P1.** Where the expected regulatory and reputational loss from untraced volume exceeds the annualised cost of verification, a shared traceability system has positive value. **P2.** The value of *blockchain* over a conventional shared database depends on whether it lowers coordination and trust costs among competing firms by more than it adds in set-up and running costs.")
    h2(doc, "2.2  Supply-chain resilience and visibility")
    para(doc, "Visibility, the ability to see inventory, flows and risks across tiers, is a widely cited precondition for resilience (Christopher and Peck, 2004), and resilient firms combine flexibility and redundancy with early warning (Sheffi and Rice, 2005). In palm oil, the relevant shocks are exogenous and of three kinds: *price shocks* (crude palm oil is volatile; the Bloomberg series used here shows 17% annualised volatility over eight weeks), *policy shocks* (export restrictions and levies in producing countries, such as Indonesia’s 2022 export curbs, and EU rules that can suspend market access) and *legal or geopolitical shocks* (the May 2026 report that Russia would seize a Wilmar stake, the March 2026 conviction of a Wilmar unit executive, and the Indonesian export under-invoicing probe, all captured in Bloomberg News).")
    para(doc, "Visibility lowers the cost of these shocks in two ways. It shortens the time to identify which suppliers and plots are affected, and it widens the set of compliant substitutes that can be activated. **Proposition P3.** Firms with low visible coverage and high concentration should show higher news-based risk and need larger substitution premia under stress. Chapter 3 measures visibility with SPLC coverage; Chapter 5 prices substitution.")
    h2(doc, "2.3  The role of blockchain traceability")
    para(doc, "A blockchain is an append-only ledger replicated across participants and updated by a consensus rule, so that no single party can alter past entries unnoticed (Nakamoto, 2008). In finance, the core problem it solves is **double spending**: the same digital token cannot be used twice. The supply-chain analogue is **double claiming**: the same tonne of certified oil sold as certified to two buyers, or a certificate reused against uncertified volume. A ledger that issues a unique lot identifier at the mill and decrements it at each split or sale can make double claiming detectable and, with smart contracts, can block payment for over-claimed volume. It also provides a shared, time-stamped record that regulators and auditors can read (Kshetri, 2018; Saberi et al., 2019; Kamilaris et al., 2019). Table 2.1 contrasts public and permissioned designs.")
    para(doc, "**False certification** is harder. A ledger guarantees that an entry was not changed after it was written; it does not guarantee that the entry was true when written. A forged GPS polygon, a bribed auditor or a mislabelled truck produces a tamper-proof lie. This is the **oracle problem**: the quality of the system is set by the off-chain data sources that feed it (Caldarelli, 2020; Babich and Hilary, 2020; Cole et al., 2019). Practical designs therefore pair the ledger with independent verification (satellite deforestation alerts, scale and sensor data, third-party audits) and with governance that lets members challenge an entry. Evidence that transparency and ledger adoption can ease financing for suppliers is promising but still model-based (Chod et al., 2020).")
    table_cap(doc, 2, "Public versus permissioned blockchains for palm oil")
    table(doc, ["Dimension", "Public (permissionless)", "Permissioned (consortium)", "Implication"], [
        ["Who can write / read", "Anyone", "Vetted members; read access can include regulators", "EUDR needs identifiable, accountable operators"],
        ["Consensus and cost", "Proof-of-work or proof-of-stake; fees per transaction", "Crash- or Byzantine-fault-tolerant voting among known nodes; low marginal cost", "Millions of lot events favour permissioned"],
        ["Throughput and latency", "Limited, variable", "High, predictable", "Matters for daily mill and refinery events"],
        ["Confidentiality", "All data visible unless encrypted", "Channel or field-level privacy; commercial terms stay off-chain", "Competitors will not share prices and volumes openly"],
        ["Governance", "Diffuse; protocol changes are hard to steer", "Defined by a consortium charter", "Rules for membership, disputes and exit can be written"],
        ["Typical role here", "Optional public anchoring of hashes for independent time-stamping", "System of record for lots, balances and escrow", "Recommended core: permissioned, with optional public anchor"],
    ], [2.8, 3.9, 4.9, 4.8], 8, ["l", "l", "l", "l"], bold_first_col=True)
    note(doc, "Source: authors’ synthesis of Kshetri (2018), Saberi et al. (2019) and Babich and Hilary (2020).")
    h2(doc, "2.4  From concepts to measurable variables")
    para(doc, "Table 2.2 shows how each construct is measured in the following chapters. The key discipline is to keep *what Bloomberg can observe* separate from *what the model assumes*.")
    table_cap(doc, 2, "Operationalisation of the framework")
    table(doc, ["Construct", "Proxy in this report", "Source", "Used in"], [
        ["Information asymmetry / visibility", "Visible supplier cost as % of COGS; number of palm nodes visible; supplier-flag shares", "Bloomberg SPLC", "Ch. 3.1, 4.1"],
        ["Supplier concentration", "HHI of visible supplier cost shares", "SPLC (computed)", "Ch. 3.1, 4.1"],
        ["Disclosure versus footprint", "BESG score; Scope 1 and Scope 3 emissions", "Bloomberg ESG", "Ch. 3.3, 4.1"],
        ["Reputational and legal risk", "Hand-coded severity of CN headlines (12 months)", "Bloomberg CN", "Ch. 3.3, 4.1"],
        ["Commodity exposure", "KOZ6 price and volatility; gross-margin trend", "Bloomberg GP/HP, FA", "Ch. 3.2, 5"],
        ["Resilience buffer", "Interest cover; inventory days", "Bloomberg FA", "Ch. 3.2, 4.1"],
        ["Verification cost and benefit", "Programme CapEx/OpEx, coverage, effectiveness, fines, premia", "ASSUMPTION", "Ch. 5"],
    ], [3.8, 6.4, 3.1, 3.1], 8.5, ["l", "l", "l", "l"], bold_first_col=True)
